xits). Unlike most of this channel's titles it carries no dollar figure, no 'you' and no personal threshold; the viewer must already know what private credit is to feel addressed. Same-channel macro/news-warning titles cluster in low-to-mid TYPICAL: HR6wU9Hb6xk 'Wall Street Just Gave a Dire Warning (You're Not Ready)' 1.21959x TYPICAL, v7uG773-wAY 'The Beginning of the US Debt Collapse is Here' 1.11089x TYPICAL, QlPqXGV491Q 'WTF Is Happening To The Stock Market?!' 1.20385x TYPICAL, TIrIi752GSc 'The Bond Market Is Breaking - Here's What It Does to YOUR Mortgage!' 0.61547x TYPICAL, mErWx9GiVUc 'You're Not Ready For What's Coming' 0.77667x TYPICAL, 1W9dzLgpU4k 'What The Average Person Should Do Before The Market Crash' 0.54346x TYPICAL. Counterexamples: bv5qS6sR4Bs 'Are You Ready For What's Coming?' 4.79096x STRONG (vague threat title that broke out) and Dgp4_ehMo9k 'If You Don't Understand Bonds, You Don't Understand Money' 13.70542x STRONG, an evergreen explainer of a 'boring' fixed-income asset framed as a personal knowledge gap rather than as news. At 1.19071x this video sits in the same band as the other news-warning titles; the descriptive pattern is that asset-class news framing lands TYPICAL on this channel while personal-gap framing of a similar asset class has broken out once. Eight titles; not causal."", ""peer_comparison_ids"": [""HR6wU9Hb6xk"", ""v7uG773-wAY"", ""TIrIi752GSc"", ""QlPqXGV491Q"", ""mErWx9GiVUc"", ""1W9dzLgpU4k"", ""bv5qS6sR4Bs"", ""Dgp4_ehMo9k""], ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_TITLE"", ""script_alignment"": ""Strongly aligned. 'Rushing For the Exits' is paid off with specific redemption-queue mechanics (quarterly repurchase offers, 5% cap, proration, Alvin asking for $80,000 and receiving about $40,000), and 'Panic' with the First Brands/Tricolor collapses and Dimon's 'cockroach' remark. The script is more balanced than 'Panic' implies: it gives a steel-man section (direct lending index returns, locked funds, Blackstone's gates-as-feature argument) and closes with 'Not that private credit is a fraud because mostly it is not.'"", ""search_demand"": ""Moderate topical search interest is plausible given 2025-2026 trade and mainstream coverage of non-traded BDC redemption queues (see event block), but 'private credit panic' is a news phrase rather than an evergreen query. Historical query volume, Google Trends and traffic sources were not fetched."", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:R1AcuUo916k"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:R1AcuUo916k"", ""audit-batches/NICK_152_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""Registered maxresdefault fetched fresh and inspected at native 1280x720 and 320x180. White background. Left third: the channel-style cartoon man (large egg-shaped head, thinning brown hair, white polo shirt, blue jeans) with both hands pressed to his cheeks, wide eyes and clenched, grimacing teeth, a fear/dread expression. Top right: very large black condensed uppercase text 'ITS COMING...' on two lines (no apostrophe in 'ITS'). Lower right: a small scene of four cartoon men in dark suits and ties, mouths open, running out of a gray door under a red 'EXIT' sign, one carrying a brown briefcase; behind them a white document labeled 'PRIVATE CREDIT' in black with a red zig-zag arrow falling to the lower right. Hierarchy: the headline text first, then the frightened face, then the exit scene. At 320px 'ITS COMING...' remains clearly legible and the face reads as alarmed; the red EXIT sign and running crowd still register as a group, but the 'PRIVATE CREDIT' label is very small and only marginally readable, so on mobile the image communicates generic dread ('something is coming') rather than the specific subject. Relation to title and script: the running-businessmen-through-EXIT scene illustrates 'Rushing For the Exits' literally, and the falling arrow matches 'Panic'; the image text does not repeat the title and adds a vague time-threat. However, the script's actual protagonists are a retired machinist (Alvin)