fore the narrator introduces himself at about 906 characters. A self-diagnosis checklist ('Do you have a target date fund with a year printed in the name? ... a pension?') converts an institutional topic into a personal one, which the title does not do. The single organizing idea is stated explicitly and early: 'Everything you own has two prices' (appraisal versus what someone will hand you this afternoon), and every later section is tied back to it ('Remember your two prices'), so the explainer has a spine rather than a list. Transitions work through callbacks: the windshield-wiper company planted at about 3,600 ('Hold that thought') pays off as First Brands at about 4,100; Alvin's queue is returned to after each mechanism. Analogies are concrete and household (trade-in pricing, house appraisal versus your street, a smoke detector with the battery out, telling a spouse the money is 'fine, just not available'). A strong structural choice is the steel-man section ('let me argue against myself'), which concedes the index returns, locked-fund structure and Blackstone's gate argument, then turns it into a sharper point: the gate 'moves the loss onto whoever needs their money most urgently'. Weaknesses: (1) many figures are given without attribution or date (12% average request, $15 billion, 53%, 8% PIK share, 2.8% non-accruals, 13% of bank lending), so viewers cannot check them; (2) the Alvin/Karina/Erica stories are presented as real ('That happened to a retired machinist named Alvin') with no sourcing, so they should be treated as illustrative and UNVERIFIED; (3) the '5% cap means a 5-year exit' arithmetic is a linear simplification (see claim audit); (4) two mid-video calls to action (subscribe 'without a commission', a school community link) interrupt but are short. The payoff is practical and proportionate: read repurchase terms now, plan cash needs around a 5% cap, and treat a smooth line as absence of pricing rather than safety. All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification."", ""audio_verified"": false, ""availability"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""beat_map"": [{""beat"": ""Cold open: 11 years of smooth gains, asks for $80,000 in March, receives about $40,000 three months later; 'nothing was stolen'; both numbers 'real'"", ""character_range"": [0, 906]}, {""beat"": ""Nick intro, private credit as about $1.7 trillion outside banks and markets, subscribe line, self-diagnosis checklist, introduces Karina and Erica"", ""character_range"": [906, 1984]}, {""beat"": ""Thesis: 'Everything you own has two prices' (appraisal versus cash-out price) with house and stock analogies"", ""character_range"": [1984, 2570]}, {""beat"": ""History: post-2008 rules push loans from banks to funds; 'a bank with better branding and worse disclosure'; windshield-wiper plant; about 30% of below-investment-grade debt, up from 13"", ""character_range"": [2570, 3995]}, {""beat"": ""Trigger events: First Brands (>$10 billion liabilities, about $2.3 billion receivables missing, loan to 33 cents) and Tricolor (about $1.9 billion), JPMorgan $170 million write-off, Dimon 'cockroach' quote"", ""character_range"": [3995, 5489]}, {""beat"": ""Redemption mechanics: retail funds from $2,500, quarterly repurchase offers, 5% cap with proration, 12% average requests, $15 billion asked and about 53% paid, Blue Owl 22% and tech fund over 40%, '5 years to leave', Karina's enrollment email, Erica as system not villain"", ""character_range"": [5489, 8063]}, {""beat"": ""Two prices in practice: Blue Owl merger exchange ratio near 80 cents, deal collapses in 48 hours; listed BDCs about 25% below book, Prospect Capital near 57% discount"", ""character_range"": [8063, 9481]}, {""beat"": ""Cliff Asness 'volatility laundering'; smoothing only postpones bad news because a loan has no upside surprise"", ""character_range"": [9481, 10193]}, {""beat"": ""Emotional texture of a redemption queue: portal every 90 days, $18,000 roof quote versus $201,000 ba