mmonly reported dates (Tricolor about 10 September 2025, First Brands about 28 September 2025) would give 18 days, not 19, but the exact dates were not confirmed in this review's searches, so the 19-day figure is UNVERIFIED. The 'about $2.3 billion' figure matches reporting of a court filing; '$10 billion in liabilities', 'inflated 50 times' and '33 cents' were not checked."", ""'JPMorgan wrote off $170 million ... When you see one cockroach, there are probably more': confirmed by secondary coverage of JPMorgan's Q3 2025 earnings call (October 2025); JPMorgan's $170 million charge was tied to Tricolor. The narrator's phrase 'the SEC now says substantially did not exist' was not checked against an SEC filing; UNVERIFIED."", ""'This year, across the 12 largest of these funds, the average request to leave hit 12%. Investors like you asked for $15 billion and got back about 53% of it': secondary/trade coverage reports total non-traded BDC redemption requests of more than $15 billion in Q1 2026 (Robert A. Stanger and Co. data) with about $7 billion returned, roughly 47% (analyst calculation 7/15), and one aggregator cites a 53% Q1 fulfilment rate; Q2 2026 requests were reported at about $15.6 billion with $5.9 billion paid (about 38%). Fitch reported Q3 requests averaging 9.8% across 13 of 15 perpetual BDCs, down from 10.3% in Q2 (Q3 data post-dates publication). The narrator's figures are broadly consistent with Q1 2026 but the '12 largest' and '12%' framing was not matched to a named source; treat as PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED. Primary Stanger data not opened."", ""'You get 5% a quarter, so it takes you 5 years to leave': 20 quarters x 5% = 100% only if each quarter you receive 5% of your original holding. If the fund repurchases 5% of shares outstanding and every holder requests 100%, each holder receives 5% of the remaining balance each quarter, so after 20 quarters about 35.8% is still unredeemed (0.95^20 = 0.358, analyst calculation) and full exit never mathematically completes. If only a minority request, exit is faster. The narrator correctly says this is 'the flattering version', but the 5-year figure is a simplification, not a ceiling."", ""'Alvin asked for 80,000 and got half': $40,000 / $80,000 = 50% (analyst calculation), consistent with the stated 53% average fulfilment. Alvin, Karina and Erica are presented as real people with no sourcing; UNVERIFIED and likely composite or illustrative."", ""'Last November, Blue Owl proposed merging two of its own funds ... roughly 80 cents on the dollar ... The deal collapsed in 48 hours': the OBDC/OBDC II merger was terminated effective 18 November 2025 and announced 19 November 2025 (SEC 8-K exhibit; trade coverage), citing market conditions, with reports that OBDC II holders faced a redemption pause and potential losses from OBDC trading below NAV. The 'roughly 80 cents' exchange-ratio estimate and the '48 hours' timing were not confirmed in this review; the merger had been announced earlier in November per secondary coverage. Later '$1.4 billion of assets ... wind the fund down' UNVERIFIED."", ""'One Prospect Capital disclosed a discount near 57%. ... offering you 43 cents': arithmetic consistent (1 - 0.57 = 0.43). The discount figure and the group-wide '25% below stated book value' were not checked; UNVERIFIED."", ""'Last August, an executive order told the Labor Department ... and this March, it proposed the rule handing your employer a safe harbor': confirmed. Executive Order 14330, signed 7 August 2025; DOL proposed 'Fiduciary Duties in Selecting Designated Investment Alternatives' on 30 March 2026 with comments due 1 June 2026 (law-firm summaries: Morrison Foerster, Gibson Dunn, Sullivan and Cromwell). Note the proposal is described as asset-neutral and process-based; it does not mandate private credit, so 'it arrives inside your target date fund' is a plausible pathway, not a rule requirement."", ""'You pay around 3.3% a year on the retail version. You would pay three to 20 basis points for an index fund. That is three percentage points of drag': 3.30% minus 0.20% = 3.10 points; minus 0.03% = 3.27 points (analyst calculation), so 'three percentage points' is consistent with the narrator's own inputs. The 3.3% total-cost figure is UNVERIFIED and varies by fund and share class (management, incentive, servicing and interest expense are often combined in such totals)."", ""'A hundred thousand dollars five years ago is about a hundred eighty-one thousand today in the total United States stock market. ... 156 ... 122': implied annualized returns are about 12.6%, 9.3% and 4.1% respectively (analyst calculation). The underlying index and fund return series were not fetched; UNVERIFIED. Because the period is unspecified ('five years ago' from August 2026) and the private credit fund is unnamed, the comparison cannot be reproduced."", ""'The main direct lending index returned 9.3% last year, has averaged 9 and 1/2 over 20 years, and posted exactly one negative year in 2008': likely refers to the Cliffwater Direct Lending Index; not checked; UNVERIFIED."", ""'Fitch tracked about 300 of these companies and found a default rate over 9% last year'; 'non-accruals ... 2.8% ... highest since 2017'; 'PIK ... around 8% of interest income'; 'close to 30% of all below investment grade corporate debt ... up from 13'; '13% of all bank lending, up from 4% in 2015'; '1.7 trillion dollars': none checked in this review; UNVERIFIED. These are plausible industry statistics but the script cites no source or date for most of them."", ""'In late 2022, Blackstone's real estate version gated for 16 straight months ... by early 2024, it was paying everybody again': BREIT began limiting redemptions in December 2022 and reported fulfilling all requests in early 2024 (reviewer background knowledge; not re-checked in this review), so the timeline is plausible but UNVERIFIED here."", ""Possible ASR artefacts preserved, not corrected against audio: 'And the the gap', 'the answer 