y owned object (your credit score) and a common frustration, so it is clearer than the channel's abstract mindset titles. It has no number, dollar figure or stakes marker (the script's strongest payoffs, the 22-point drop and the claimed $31,200 mortgage cost, are absent from the title), and it reads like a search-style FAQ rather than a surprising reveal. Same-channel comparisons: the closest topical sibling, 4eGrsL5CwBQ 'Why Everything Changes After You Get 832 Credit Score', published three days earlier, landed at 0.54383x TYPICAL, so two credit-score titles in one week both sat near the WEAK/TYPICAL boundary. Other 'Why Your [money object]' explainers are also low: J_sTOWazmho 'Why Your $1,000 Car Payment Actually Costs $4,000 (The Hidden Math)' 0.50669x TYPICAL despite a dollar figure; debt how-to r4fpD0dgQ64 'Fast Way to Pay Off $10K in Debt on a Small Salary' 0.2221x WEAK; gwm1Hr5iipU 'The 50 Year Mortgage Trap That'll Keep You in Debt Forever' 0.32481x WEAK. Counterexamples where 'Why Your' worked attach the question to a life-stage or wealth aspiration: mWhpJZrTyNU 'Why Your First Million Feels Impossible (And The Next Comes Fast)' 2.07599x STRONG and ZnI6pKMnpf4 'Why Your 40s Beat Your 20s for Building Wealth' 1.68072x TYPICAL; HEtVmp38D4M 'Invest or Pay Off Debt? The Wealthy Know the Answer' 3.64145x STRONG frames debt as a wealth decision; tynTfEi4518 'The Car Payment Trap Nobody Talks About' 24.51666x STRONG frames a debt object as a hidden trap. Within this small sample, credit/debt remediation framing co-occurs with weak results and aspiration or trap framing with stronger ones; this is descriptive across eight titles, not a proven cause."", ""peer_comparison_ids"": [""4eGrsL5CwBQ"", ""J_sTOWazmho"", ""r4fpD0dgQ64"", ""gwm1Hr5iipU"", ""mWhpJZrTyNU"", ""ZnI6pKMnpf4"", ""HEtVmp38D4M"", ""tynTfEi4518""], ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_TITLE"", ""script_alignment"": ""Well aligned at the start: the cold open is exactly the promised scenario (bills paid in full, score falls 22 points from 738 to 716 on utilization). The script then broadens to seven traps (utilization snapshot, limit cuts, hard inquiries, closing old cards, paying off an installment loan, authorized-user late payments, score-version mismatch) plus report errors and a bureau business-model segment. Some sections (inquiries, report errors, multiple score versions) are not really 'drops while paying on time', so the title undersells the breadth and the Thomas mortgage-cost payoff."", ""search_demand"": ""Plausible explicit search intent ('why did my credit score drop', 'credit score dropped even though I paid on time' are natural queries), so the title is closer to search phrasing than most channel titles. Historical query volume, search traffic share and impressions are unavailable."", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""Registered maxresdefault fetched fresh and inspected at native 1280x720 and proportional 320x180. Pure white background. Left half: a heavy-set cartoon man with ginger hair, furrowed angry brows, narrowed eyes and an open shouting mouth, wearing a blue hoodie, holding a smartphone in both hands. The phone screen shows the small black words 'CREDIT SCORE' above a red zig-zag arrow pointing downward. Top right: very large black condensed uppercase text 'STILL' on one line and 'DROPS?!' below it. A thick hand-drawn black curved arrow sweeps from under the text down and left toward the phone. Hierarchy: 'STILL DROPS?!' first, then the angry face, then the phone. At 320px 'STILL DROPS?!' stays clearly legible with high black-on-white contrast and the angry expression remains readable; the 'CREDIT SCORE' label on the phone becomes tiny and only marginally readable, while the red down-arrow remains visible as a color accent. So on mobile the topic (credit score) is carried mainly by the title, not the image. The image text does not repeat the title; 'STILL DROPS?!' depends on the title's 'Even When You Pay On Time' to make sense, which is a coherent title-thumbnail split, and the anger matches the script's stated aim that the viewer will be 'genuinely angry at a system'. No number (716, 22 points, $31,200) is shown. Same generic white-background cartoon-plus-arrow template seen on other channel thumbnails; historical continuity, A/B testing and CTR contribution are unverified."", ""coverage"": {""Association_verified"": ""NO"", ""Historical_design"": ""UNKNOWN"", ""Local_asset"": """", ""Mobile_review"": ""NOT_REVIEWED"", ""Thumbnail_URL"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/RUVOjfrIemY/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""URL_status"": ""PRESENT_NOT_FETCHED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RUVOjfrIemY""}, ""dimensions"": [1280, 720], ""historical_design"": ""UNKNOWN"", ""image_path"": ""research/finance-economics-intelligence/audit-batches/NICK_RUVOjfrIemY.jpg"", ""image_sha256"": ""9f1aaeba346d164b2780eec6b5e92212a28bd3104d11a2f8d07fb241b5c48250"", ""image_source"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/RUVOjfrIemY/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""mobile_review_width"": 320, ""performance_effect"": ""NOT_ESTABLISHED"", ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_IMAGE"", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""THUMBNAIL_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""Full 18,406 canonical characters read in two contiguous chunks (0-10,000 and 10,000-18,406). The hook is a second-person micro-scene (Credit Karma on a Tuesday morning, expecting 738, seeing 716, refreshing, scrolling for a red flag, finding only 'your utilization increased'), which is far more concrete than the channel's usual mindset openings and directly pays the title within the first 1,000 characters. It ends with an escalation ('this isn't even the most counterintuitive way your score can fall') and an emotional promise ('you're going to be genuinely angry'). A second hook follows: the Thomas case study (34, 12 years of on-time payments, mortgage meeting Thursday, expected 740+, has 711) with an open loop ('I'll tell you what that 29-point gap is going to actually cost him in dollars ... a number that'll make you put your phone down'). This running-character device is the script's best structural choice: Thomas is called back after the utilization trap (2,800 dollar Chicago trip, 9% to 31%, -22 points), after the loan payoff trap (-14 points), after the score-version section (Credit Karma 738 vs mortgage FICO 711), and then the loop is closed with a dollar figure. Each trap is introduced by a labelled header line ('The utilization trap', 'Hard inquiries, small cuts that stack up', 'Closing your oldest card, a self-inflicted wound', 'The cruel paradox of paying off your loan'), which works as spoken chapter cards and keeps a list structure without numbering. Worked micro-examples with numbers (10,000 limit cut to 7,000; 18,000 to 8,000 after closing a card) make the mechanics tangible and all of that arithmetic checks out. Weaknesses: (1) Thomas's story is internally inconsistent: the summary says he 'closed his oldest card', which never appears in his earlier beats, and the gap is stated as 29 points (740 expected vs 711) and later 738 vs 711 (27 points); (2) the climactic $31,200 figure is understated by the narrator's own inputs (about $36,600 by standard amortization), so the payoff number is wrong in the conservative direction; (3) sarcasm beats ('Very cool. Very fair.', 'MySpace profile') are frequent and a 'Who actually benefits from your confusion' segment turns explanatory content into a business-model grievance, which the narrator himself hedges ('This isn't a conspiracy'); (4) several mechanics are stated as fixed point losses (close a card, -28; pay off a car, -15 or 10 to 25; Thomas -14) when FICO does not publish such deltas. The close is unusually actionable: five steps in 48 hours (pull all three reports at annualcreditreport.com, pay before statement close date to below 10%, keep old cards with a small autopay charge, ask which FICO version the lender uses and buy it, review authorized-user status), followed by a disclaimer and a subscribe/comment CTA. A mid-roll subscribe ask also appears in the intro. All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification."", ""audio_verified"": false, ""availability"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""beat_map"": [{""beat"": ""Cold open: Credit Karma 738 expected, 716 shown, 'your utilization increased', 22-point drop after paying in full; 'system working exactly as designed'; Nick intro, subscribe ask, 'genuinely angry' promise"", ""character_range"": [0, 1572]}, {""beat"": ""Thomas introduced: 34, 12 years no missed payments, mortgage pre-approval Thursday, expected 740+, sees 711; open loop on what the 29-point gap costs in dollars"", ""character_range"": [1572, 2368]}, {""beat"": ""Report-card myth vs FICO five categories (35/30/15/10/10); 'the other 65% is a minefield'"", ""character_range"": [2368, 3017]}, {""beat"": ""Utilization trap: 30% is a cliff, 850 scorers average 4.1%, statement-close-date snapshot vs due date, 2,800 dollar work trip example, 'photograph of your wallet' metaphor; Thomas callback 9% to 31%, -22 points"", ""character_range"": [3017, 5194]}, {""beat"": ""Credit-limit cut variant: 10,000 to 7,000 available, 2,000 balance goes from 20% to 28.6%"", ""character_range"": [5194, 5738]}, {""beat"": ""Hard inquiries: under five points each, six or more inquiries up to eight times bankruptcy risk, pre-qualification soft pull vs formal application hard pull, 2.8 billion pre-approval offers in 2024"", ""character_range"": [5738, 6912]}, {""beat"": ""Closing oldest card: -28 points example, 18,000 to 8,000 limit math (20% to 45%), closed accounts count up to 10 years, keep open with 12 dollar subscription or product change"", ""character_range"": [6912, 8402]}, {""beat"": ""Paying off the last installment loan: credit mix 10%, 10 to 25 point temporary drop; Thomas callback, car loan paid 3 months before meeting, -14 points"", ""character_range"": [8402, 9662]}, {""beat"": ""Authorized users: inherit age and history, partner's 30-day late can cost 60 to 110 points and stays 7 years; removal drops account in 30 to 60 days"", ""character_range"": [9662, 10799]}, {""beat"": ""Score versions: 19 FICO versions, mortgage FICO 2/4/5, cards FICO 8, auto 250-900, Credit Karma VantageScore 3.0; Urban Institute 14-point gap claim; 750 vs 712 example; Thomas callback 738 vs 711"", ""character_range"": [10799, 12309]}, {""beat"": ""Report errors: FTC 1,001-person study, one in four, 40 million extrapolation; e-OSCAR 29 codes, four codes over 90%; CFPB Experian 'sham investigations' suit and Equifax 15 million dollar penalty, January 2025"", ""character_range"": [12309, 13887]}, {""beat"": ""Who benefits: bureau and lender business-model loop, hedged as 'not a conspiracy'"", ""character_range"": [13887, 14847]}, {""beat"": ""Thomas payoff: needed 740 for best tier, 7.1% vs 6.7% on 380,000 dollars over 30 years = 31,200 dollars; recap of his mistakes; apartment 720 cutoff, car loan 9.2% vs 5.8%, insurance credit pulls"", ""character_range"": [14847, 16254]}, {""beat"": ""Five actions in 48 hours: three free reports and written disputes, pay before statement close to under 10%, keep old cards, ask lender's FICO version and buy it on myFICO for about 20 dollars, review authorized-user status"", ""character_range"": [16254, 18038]}, {""beat"": ""Not-financial-advice disclaimer; subscribe and comment CTA"", ""character_range"": [18038, 18406]}], ""claim_audit"": [""'Payment history is 35%, amounts owed is 30%, length of credit history is 15%, credit mix is 10%. New credit is 10%': matches FICO's published general weighting for the general population (myFICO credit education); FICO describes these as approximate and population-dependent, not fixed per person. Not reopened in this review beyond prior knowledge; treat as consistent with FICO documentation."", ""'Your card issuer reports your balance to the credit bureaus once a month, almost always on your statement close date': directionally consistent with how most issuers report, but reporting dates are set by each issuer and not uniformly the statement date; 'almost always' is UNVERIFIED as a statistic. The advice to pay before the statement close date is sound."", ""'People who actually carry 850 FICO scores have average utilization of just 4.1%': UNVERIFIED. A search did not locate this figure in Experian or FICO material; Experian's State of Credit reports found average revolving utilization of about 26-30% for all consumers (2017-2020). The figure may come from a different, unidentified source."", ""'Cross 50% and you can lose 50 to 100 points': no FICO source provides point losses by utilization band; UNVERIFIED and likely an illustrative range."", ""Utilization arithmetic (analyst recalculation): 2,000/7,000 = 28.57% (narrator 28.6%, correct); 3,600/18,000 = 20% and 3,600/8,000 = 45% (correct). Thomas's 9% to 31% jump from 2,800 dollars implies a total limit of about 12,727 dollars and a prior balance of about 1,145 dollars, internally plausible but not stated."", ""'A single one costs fewer than five points' and 'consumers with six or more inquiries are up to eight times more likely to declare bankruptcy': both match myFICO's inquiries page ('less than five points' for most people; 'six inquiries or more ... up to eight times more likely to declare bankruptcy than people with no inquiries'). This is a statistical association, not causation; the script omits FICO's rate-shopping rule (mortgage, auto and student-loan inquiries within a 45-day window count as one in newer FICO versions), which is the single most relevant inquiry fact for Thomas's mortgage shopping."", ""'Banks sent an estimated 2.8 billion pre-approval offers in 2024': UNVERIFIED; no source searched or cited."", ""'FICO still counts the closed account for up to 10 years': consistent with common bureau practice for closed accounts in good standing; not reopened in this review. Fixed losses ('drops 28 points', 'drops 15 points', '10 to 25 points', Thomas '14 points') are illustrative and not published by FICO; UNVERIFIED."", ""'A single 30-day late payment on a previously clean profile can drop a FICO score by 60 to 110 points' and 'stays there for 7 years': the 7-year reporting limit for late payments is consistent with the Fair Credit Reporting Act; the 60-110 point range resembles older FICO illustrative simulations but was not verified here. 'The algorithm doesn't distinguish between primary holders and authorized users' is overstated: FICO 8 and later include authorized-user logic, and lenders may treat it differently; UNVERIFIED as stated."", ""'FICO alone produces 19 commonly used versions' and 'Mortgage lenders currently use FICO 2, FICO 4, and FICO 5': mortgage use of Classic FICO (FICO 2/4/5) for GSE-conforming loans is broadly consistent with practice through 2025; FHFA has announced moves toward FICO 10T and VantageScore 4.0, so 'currently' was in transition at publication. A verification search for the FHFA announcement failed (search session limit), so the version count and the transition status are UNVERIFIED in this review. 'Auto lenders use FICO auto scores on a 250 to 900 scale' is consistent with FICO industry-score ranges. Credit Karma showing VantageScore 3.0 is consistent with Credit Karma's own disclosures (not reopened)."", ""'Urban Institute research found Vantage Score runs about 14 points higher than classic FICO scores on average': UNVERIFIED; no source opened."", ""'The FTC completed a landmark study where 1,001 people reviewed their own credit reports ... One in four found an error significant enough to potentially affect their score': supported by the FTC's February 2013 report to Congress (1,001 consumers; one in four identified errors that might affect scores). The narrator omits the more decision-relevant FTC finding that about 5% had errors that could lead to less favorable loan terms, and only about one in 20 had a maximum score change above 25 points. 'Roughly 40 million Americans with meaningful errors ... right now' is an extrapolation of 2013 data and is not an FTC estimate; presenting it as current overstates certainty."", ""'E-Oscar ... one of 29 three-digit codes' and 'A congressional investigation found the same four codes were used in over 90% of disputes': the e-OSCAR code-compression critique is documented by consumer advocates and the CFPB, but the specific 'congressional investigation' attribution is UNVERIFIED (the four-codes statistic is more often attributed to National Consumer Law Center research); treat as unconfirmed attribution."", ""'In January 2025, the CFPB sued Experian for conducting what they literally called sham investigations. Equifax was ordered to pay a $15 million penalty the same month': supported. The CFPB sued Experian in early January 2025 alleging 'sham investigations' of disputes, and about ten days later ordered Equifax to pay a 15 million dollar civil money penalty for improper dispute investigations (CFPB newsroom). Both were available before the 2026-04-08 publication. 'This is a system that profits when you don't know your report is wrong' and the bureau business-model loop are narrator opinion, not established findings."", ""'His rate came in at 7.1% instead of 6.7% on a $380,000 loan over 30 years, that 0.4% difference is $31,200': arithmetic error. Analyst calculation with standard monthly amortization: payment about 2,553.72 dollars at 7.1% vs 2,452.06 dollars at 6.7%, a difference of about 101.67 dollars a month or about 36,599 dollars over 360 payments (total interest about 539,340 vs 502,740). The narrator's 31,200 corresponds to about 86.67 dollars a month and is understated by about 5,400 dollars; the direction of the claim (a meaningful cost) holds. Whether a 711 vs 740 score produces a 0.4-point rate gap is lender- and date-specific and not verified; the earlier 'that 38-point gap ... can mean $30,000 to $40,000' is consistent with this order of magnitude for similar loan sizes."", ""Internal inconsistencies in the Thomas case: the gap is introduced as '29-point' (expected north of 740 minus 711) but later his monitored Credit Karma score is 738 (27 points); his recap says he 'closed his oldest card to feel responsible', an event never described in his story; his named losses (-22 utilization, -14 loan payoff) sum to 36 points and do not reconcile with either gap, though different score models are also involved."", ""'It's the car loan at 9.2% instead of 5.8% because you were in the wrong tier by 11 points' and 'apartment ... cutoff was 720': hypothetical illustrations, no source; UNVERIFIED. 'Your insurance rate quietly going up ... because they pulled your credit': credit-based insurance scores are used in most states but banned or restricted in some (for example California, Hawaii, Massachusetts); stated without that caveat."", ""Action steps: 'annualcreditreport.com ... Free' is correct (the official free-report site). 'dispute in writing ... A paper trail gives you legal standing that clicking a button does not' overstates: online disputes are also disputes under the FCRA; written disputes create better documentation but are not a separate legal standing. 'The account drops off your report within 60 days' after authorized-user removal and 'The $20 it costs' on myFICO are not verified."", ""Possible ASR artefacts preserved, not corrected against audio: 'car car was your only active installment account' (doubled word), 'Vantage Score' (spelled VantageScore), 'E-Oscar' (e-OSCAR), 'Nobody tells you about the number one silent killer' (sentence boundary after the header 'The utilization trap. Nobody tells you about' is unclear), and 'cost you a better interest rate' (likely 'costs').""], ""ending_words"": ""If you found this useful, subscribe. I do this kind of breakdown every week and if this video just saved you from making one of these mistakes, drop it in the comments. I genuinely like hearing when this stuff actually helps someone."", ""opening_words"": ""You open Credit Karma on a Tuesday morning. Not because anything's wrong, just a quick check before work. You're expecting to see the 738 you had last month, maybe 740."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_STORED_TEXT"", ""source"": {""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::144_RUVOjfrIemY_Why_Your_Credit_Score_Drops_Even_When_You_Pay_On_Time.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""eb73f3f2a20f0e438d170cb582eedd85056d14f268c2bd7778f3fcc3da176d5a"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RUVOjfrIemY"", ""Words"": ""3214""}, ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json""], ""stored_text_completeness"": ""FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio unverified"", ""timing"": ""OPENING_WORDS_ONLY""}","{""analysis"": ""Credit-score mechanics explainer: why a score falls without missed payments (utilization snapshot timing, limit cuts, hard inquiries, closing old accounts, credit-mix change after paying off a loan, authorized-user exposure, score-version mismatch, report errors). Evergreen, broad household relevance (anyone with a card or planning a mortgage), beginner-to-intermediate depth. The distinctive audience question is 'I did everything right, why did my number fall?' and the practical answer (pay before statement close, keep old cards, check the lender's FICO version, pull all three reports) is concrete and correct in direction. It departs from the channel's dominant mindset and wealth-psychology content toward consumer-credit service content."", ""competition"": ""Same-channel credit/debt service titles sit low: 4eGrsL5CwBQ 832 credit score 0.54383x TYPICAL, J_sTOWazmho car payment hidden math 0.50669x TYPICAL, r4fpD0dgQ64 pay off 10K debt 0.2221x WEAK, gwm1Hr5iipU 50-year mortgage trap 0.32481x WEAK. Aspiration or trap framings do better: mWhpJZrTyNU 2.07599x STRONG, ZnI6pKMnpf4 1.68072x TYPICAL, HEtVmp38D4M 3.64145x STRONG, tynTfEi4518 24.51666x STRONG. External credit-education competitors (bureaus, card-review sites, large finance creators) were not sampled in this review."", ""novelty_and_corpus_angle"": ""Original opportunity: keep the running case-study device (one person, one mortgage meeting, one dollar payoff) but make the arithmetic exact and the case consistent, and anchor each mechanic to the issuer's or FICO's own documentation (statement-date reporting, the 45-day rate-shopping window the script omits, FTC's 5% material-error finding rather than the 40-million extrapolation). A stronger packaging angle may be the dollar consequence ('A 29-point drop cost him $36,000') rather than the mechanism question. Avoid fixed point-loss numbers FICO does not publish."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_TRANSCRIPT_TOPIC"", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""Age_days"": ""155.1184"", ""Format"": ""LONG_FORM"", ""Lifetime_rate_ratio"": ""0.49765"", ""Metric_snapshot_date"": ""2026-09-11"", ""Peer_Video_IDs"": ""mErWx9GiVUc|eAzo0uvSnus|zwrY8QsDTgs|4eGrsL5CwBQ|ypUI1Sv-Nhk|R_E6OOnF9e4|8rhg_K7c4O8|f6HIYQUiMoU|ccNuYRhrA0U|8LncpNMNPW8"", ""Peer_count"": ""10"", ""Performance_tier"": ""WEAK"", ""Tier_basis"": ""DESCRIPTIVE_LIFETIME_RATE"", ""evidence_kind"": ""OBSERVED_FACT"", ""methodology"": ""METHODS.md; build_baseline.compare""}","{""alternative_explanations"": [""Audience-fit hypothesis: credit-score repair content may draw a different, smaller audience than the channel's wealth-mindset core; traffic and audience data unavailable."", ""Cannibalization hypothesis: 4eGrsL5CwBQ, another credit-score title three days earlier, may have absorbed demand; impressions unavailable."", ""Packaging hypothesis: no number or dollar stakes in title or thumbnail, and the topic label on the phone is illegible on mobile; CTR unavailable."", ""Confounders: algorithmic exploration timing, the extreme housing-market outlier f6HIYQUiMoU in the same window, unknown later title or thumbnail edits.""], ""analysis"": ""Observed result: 5,344 frozen views at 155.1184 days; ten-peer ratio 0.49765x WEAK and five-peer ratio 0.47287x WEAK, both independently reproduced. The tier agrees across peer windows, but the ten-peer ratio sits just under the 0.5 boundary, so the WEAK label is near the TYPICAL edge. Likes 276 (5.16% of views) and comments 45 (0.84%), engagement rates higher than in several recent channel records reviewed in this series, which is consistent with, but does not prove, satisfied clickers. Evidence-backed interpretation: in its ten-video age window, STRONG peers were a broad net-worth trap list (8rhg_K7c4O8 4.84661x), a housing-market news reaction (f6HIYQUiMoU 33.03103x) and a comparative identity list (ypUI1Sv-Nhk 2.18459x), while the other credit-score title three days earlier (4eGrsL5CwBQ 0.54383x) also landed near the boundary; two credit-score videos in one week may have split the same narrow audience. Hypotheses: (a) a credit-score-remediation topic attracts a narrower, more search-driven audience than the channel's wealth-identity browse audience; (b) the title lacks a number or stakes marker that the channel's STRONG titles often carry; (c) the thumbnail's topic cue ('CREDIT SCORE' on the phone) is nearly illegible at mobile size; (d) audience overlap with 4eGrsL5CwBQ. Proposed experiment: in owned uploads, test the same script under a dollar-stakes package (for example a mortgage-cost title with the number in the thumbnail) against the mechanism-question package, spacing credit topics at least two weeks apart, and measure CTR, impressions, search vs browse traffic share and retention at the Thomas callbacks. None of these hypotheses is measurable here without impressions, CTR, traffic sources or retention."", ""causal_status"": ""DESCRIPTIVE_ASSOCIATION_NOT_CAUSAL"", ""channel_context"": ""WEAK in both peer windows in a week where a news reaction and broad wealth lists were STRONG; comparison is within-channel so subscriber base is roughly constant, but topic audience differs between credit service content and wealth-identity content."", ""competition"": ""mErWx9GiVUc 0.77667x TYPICAL; eAzo0uvSnus 0.72657x TYPICAL; zwrY8QsDTgs 1.86362x TYPICAL; 4eGrsL5CwBQ 0.54383x TYPICAL; ypUI1Sv-Nhk 2.18459x STRONG; R_E6OOnF9e4 0.59678x TYPICAL; 8rhg_K7c4O8 4.84661x STRONG; f6HIYQUiMoU 33.03103x STRONG; ccNuYRhrA0U 1.00027x TYPICAL; 8LncpNMNPW8 0.31287x WEAK. Three of ten age peers are STRONG, six TYPICAL, and this video is one of two WEAK in the window alongside 8LncpNMNPW8."", ""format_fit"": ""20:12 long-form explainer with a running case study, labelled trap sections and a five-step action close; cartoon thumbnail; runtime is not watch time."", ""observed_result"": ""5,344 views; 276 likes; 45 comments; 1,212 seconds; 0.49765x WEAK ten-peer / 0.47287x WEAK five-peer. Public metrics only."", ""original_content_lesson"": {""confidence"": ""HIGH for mortgage and utilization arithmetic and for the FTC, myFICO inquiry and CFPB enforcement checks; MEDIUM for the within-channel descriptive pattern; LOW for causal virality attribution."", ""test"": ""Produce a credit-mechanics explainer with one consistent case study whose payoff number is exactly computed (about 36,600 dollars for 0.4 points on 380,000 dollars over 30 years), include the rate-shopping window and FTC's 5% material-error finding, and test a dollar-stakes package against a mechanism-question package in owned experiments; measure CTR, search share, and rete