approximately $171,000 at 7% and $120,000 at 10% reproduce. The title's exact $100K switch does not: at $100,000, modeled annual gains are $7,000 or $10,000, both below $12,000 contributed. Returns are volatile and not a salary; the equality is an expectation under a stated rate, not a guaranteed date."", ""Marcus milestone ladder substantially reproduces under end-of-month $1,000 deposits and nominal annual rates compounded monthly. At 7%: first $100K about 6.67 years; each next $100K about 4.42, 3.42, 2.75, 2.33, 2.00, 1.75, 1.58, 1.42 and 1.25 years. At 10%: first $100K about 6.17 years and $900K to $1M about 1.0 year. Results depend on deposit timing, compounding convention, uninterrupted contributions, taxes, fees and realized return path, none fully specified."", ""Dana's $40,000 at age 28 becoming roughly $1.3M by 65 is arithmetically plausible at a fixed 10% annual return: about $1.36M over 37 years. It is a nominal, pre-tax, pre-fee deterministic illustration; the SUV and renovation amounts do not establish that all $40,000 would otherwise remain invested."", ""$100,000 from age 30 to 65 becomes about $1.068M at 7% or $2.810M at 10%; $100,000 from age 25 to 65 becomes about $4.526M at 10%, versus $1.745M from age 35, a gap of about $2.781M. Those arithmetic examples reproduce with annual compounding but are nominal endpoint scenarios, not forecasts. The rule of 72 is an approximation and does not supply taxes, inflation, fees or volatility."", ""Federal Reserve SCF 2022 says 54.3% of families held retirement accounts, defined there as IRAs, Keoghs and certain employer-sponsored accounts. Its 45.7% complement means no account in that SCF category, not necessarily zero assets intended for retirement. Federal Reserve SHED 2022 separately reports 28% of non-retired adults had no retirement savings and notes 47% had retirement savings outside formal retirement accounts. The script's adjacent 'Roughly 46% have zero' wording is therefore ambiguous and overbroad unless explicitly limited to SCF-defined account ownership."", ""Vanguard How America Saves 2025 reports 2024 average $148,153 and median $38,176 for Vanguard defined-contribution-plan participants; the rounded numbers and skew explanation match. Vanguard cautions that current DC balances are only a partial measure of retirement preparedness, and about 75% of participants were below the average. These are not balances for all Americans, all households or all retirement assets."", ""The 2024 Census median household income available before publication was $83,730, supporting 'about $84,000'. BEA's latest available personal-saving-rate release before the 2026-06-09 upload reported 2.6% for April 2026, not 3.5%; March was 3.6%. The script may be using an older/revised month without naming it. At $84,000 income and 3.5%, $245 monthly reaches $100K in about 19 years only around a 6% nominal annual return; the rate, compounding, tax and contribution timing are omitted. With no return it takes about 34 years."", ""NYU professor Aswath Damodaran's 1928-2025 total-return series reports an 11.85% arithmetic average and 26 negative years out of 98, supporting the script's broad 'around 10%' and 'one out of every four years negative' statements. An arithmetic average is not the same as compounded investor return. The script's exact 'five years between 8 and 12%' count and 2000-2009 'basically nothing' statement were not independently reproduced from a frozen primary table in this review; retain as UNVERIFIED rather than treating them as established."", ""The $100,000 mid-1990s to about $211,000 current-dollar comparison is sensitive to the chosen starting month/year and the current CPI month. BLS confirms its calculator uses calendar-year average CPI and the latest monthly index for the current year, but the script gives only 'mid-90s' and no calculation date; the exact $211,000 is not reproducible from the stated inputs and remains PARTIAL."", ""The 1% advisory-fee warning is directionally and conditionally plausible. SEC Investor.gov shows that ongoing fees reduce the balance left to compound. Under one transparent 35-year illustration, reducing a fixed 7% gross return to 6% lowers terminal wealth by about 28%, close to 'around a quarter'. The fraction changes with horizon, contributions, gross return and fee application, so it is not universal."", ""The Buffett '3% by age 65 / 97% after' statistic relies on age-specific net-worth estimates and changing Berkshire/share values; no primary Berkshire source for that exact decomposition was found in this review. The conclusion that long compounding time mattered is plausible, but the exact percentages and 'other fund managers have tripled his percentage' are UNSOURCED here.""], ""ending_words"": ""YouTube is honestly one of the best opportunities of 2026 if it's done right, and the door's open. Link is in the description. I'm waiting for you. Just a reminder, I'm not a financial adviser. This video is for educational purposes only and any results depend on your own decisions and actions."", ""opening_words"": ""Somewhere out there, right now, someone is checking their investment account on their lunch break, and they're about to notice something strange. Last month, they put in $1,000, same as always. But, their account grew by 2,300."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_STORED_TEXT"", ""source"": {""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::090_QtXhRiEJ9zo_Once_You_Hit_100K_The_Math_Does_The_Rest.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""6f15d49f002e5e7d96f780075ca83e74bc80f131699a89d077c9be6884014ed5"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""Words"": ""3101""}, ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QtXh