r,' so the singular framing creates a mild promise mismatch. Frozen performance is 0.57110x TYPICAL against ten peers but 0.42432x WEAK against five; public counts cannot isolate a title effect."", ""peer_comparison_ids"": [""BZAU-4_MSsA"", ""uU64zaQKd30"", ""4D90YRoT2yo"", ""SlyosXgW__4"", ""w5hm8ZXtCgw"", ""30EYlADmKpE"", ""CQO9rtzuc4E"", ""4KiTtL0OrPs"", ""XkhWSzOB7fI"", ""zutCmVPrQcU""], ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_TITLE"", ""script_alignment"": ""Strong topic and audience alignment, but the title implies a definitive answer while the conclusion correctly depends on essential expenses, income volatility, dependents, insurance, debt and access constraints."", ""search_demand"": ""Durable personal-finance intent around emergency-fund size, job-loss resilience and unexpected expenses. Historical query volume and traffic sources are unavailable."", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""audit-batches/NICK_157_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""The registered image uses a white background, a large black two-line 'THE REAL NUMBER,' a smiling cartoon presenter pointing toward a pink piggy bank, stacked coins and a green bill, plus a thick black arrow. At 320x180 the text, face, pig and arrow remain legible; the smaller bill and coin details simplify but remain recognizable. Contrast is high and clutter is low. The image complements rather than repeats the title by withholding the number, but the singular certainty is stronger than the script's individualized range. Historical upload art, CTR, impressions and A/B tests are unavailable."", ""coverage"": {""Association_verified"": ""NO"", ""Historical_design"": ""UNKNOWN"", ""Local_asset"": """", ""Mobile_review"": ""NOT_REVIEWED"", ""Thumbnail_URL"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/RmJKnJkUgxA/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""URL_status"": ""PRESENT_NOT_FETCHED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RmJKnJkUgxA""}, ""dimensions"": [1280, 720], ""historical_design"": ""UNKNOWN"", ""image_path"": ""research/finance-economics-intelligence/audit-batches/NICK_RmJKnJkUgxA.jpg"", ""image_sha256"": ""61f01b99b22e4755e5162d300491004de33c22bcdbf342e1e30db5790f2a8876"", ""image_source"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/RmJKnJkUgxA/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""mobile_review_width"": 320, ""performance_effect"": ""NOT_ESTABLISHED"", ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_IMAGE"", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""THUMBNAIL_COVERAGE.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""audit-batches/NICK_157_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""The script has a clear problem-solution progression and repeatedly relieves all-or-nothing saving anxiety. Concrete examples, rhetorical questions, recurring 'here's' pivots, numerical milestones and objection handling support retention, while the conclusion returns to the $2,000 anchor and immediate action. Its strongest substantive idea is that emergency liquidity should be sized to essential expenses and household risk, with a starter buffer before a larger income-shock reserve. Its weakest passages turn survey associations into equivalence, present old or unnamed survey figures as current national facts, treat credit availability and informal support as reliable substitutes for cash, and understate unemployment, insurance, market, transfer and product risks. The recommendations are planning heuristics, not universal rules or individualized financial advice."", ""audio_verified"": false, ""availability"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""beat_map"": [""3 a.m. car and appliance anxiety hook"", ""Challenge to the universal three-to-six-month rule"", ""$2,000 well-being claim and small-expense examples"", ""Survey statistics on $1,000 capacity, no fund and median savings"", ""Household-risk segmentation and essential-expense definition"", ""Starter-buffer and $25-per-week progress framing"", ""Reframing emergency-fund use as success followed by rebuilding"", ""Liquidity ladder using savings, money-market products and CDs"", ""Credit, sellable assets and family as backup resources"", ""Starter fund versus high-interest debt tradeoff"", ""Tiered small, medium and income-replacement funds"", ""Inflation, wages and purchasing-power warning"", ""Insurance, unemployment benefits and probability-based conclusion""], ""claim_audit"": [""SUPPORTED AS A RULE OF THUMB, NOT A UNIVERSAL RULE: Three to six months of expenses is common income-shock guidance, while CFPB says the amount depends on the person's situation. Three, six or nine months cannot be prescribed solely from family size, job type or salary without cash-flow, insurance, debt, benefits and risk information."", ""MISINTERPRETED ASSOCIATION: Vanguard estimated a 21% higher financial-well-being score for respondents with at least $2,000 of emergency savings versus 18% for $1 million-plus of assets in its regression. That does not mean $2,000 is economically 'as powerful' as $1 million, and observational association is not causal equivalence."", ""DATED PRIVATE SURVEY: Bankrate's December 2023 poll reported 44% could pay a $1,000 emergency from savings. It predates the September 2025 video by about 21 months and uses specific survey wording. Federal Reserve 2024 probability-survey results use different questions: 63% could cover $400 with cash or equivalent and 55% reported a three-month rainy-day fund."", ""UNREPRODUCED NATIONAL FIGURES: The transcript names no source for 42% having no emergency fund or a $500 median among people with emergency savings. These are not reproduced by the cited Federal Reserve measures and must not be combined across surveys as one population estimate."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED: $2,000 of essential monthly expenses times three months is $6,000. The expense base is a household decision and should include unavoidable obligations, timing and realistic reductions, not a universal list."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED: $25 per week for 52 weeks equals $1,300 before interest. The behavioral claim that this necessarily builds confidence is plausible but not established for every saver."", ""SUPPORTED CONCEPT: Using an emergency fund for a genuine shock is not fund failure; replenishment afterward is part of the plan. Whether a cost is unexpected, necessary and urgent is household-specific."", ""INCORRECT GENERALIZATION: Credit cards do not guarantee 'at least 30 days' of free financing. Statements generally must provide at least 21 days to the due date, but grace periods are not required and interest avoidance depends on product terms, transaction type, prior balance and full timely payment. Credit limits can also be reduced or unavailable during stress."", ""PRODUCT DISTINCTION REQUIRED: High-yield savings accounts and bank money-market deposit accounts can be insured deposits within applicable limits; money-market mutual funds are securities, are not FDIC-insured and can lose value. CDs may impose early-withdrawal restrictions or penalties. Transfer and settlement timing should be tested before treating any tier as emergency cash."", ""CONDITIONAL TRADEOFF: At the stated simple annual rates, 20% debt minus 1% savings is a 19-percentage-point spread, not proof that every dollar beyond $500-$1,000 should repay debt. Minimum liquidity, employer match, delinquency risk, tax, promotional rates, credit access and likely shock size can change sequencing."", ""UNVERIFIED AVERAGE: The transcript provides no reproducible source or definition for an 'average emergency' below $2,000. Spending shocks and income shocks differ in frequency, duration and severity; an average does not size tail-risk protection for a household."", ""SUPPORTED DIRECTION, UNSUPPORTED EXAMPLES: Inflation reduces the purchasing power of fixed cash and justifies periodic recalculation. The specific claims that $500 repairs became $1,000, $100 medical bills became $300 and $200 groceries became $300 are anecdotes without a defined item, geography or interval; the blanket claim that wages failed to keep pace for most people is also unsourced."", ""MATERIAL RISK OMISSION: Unemployment insurance is partial and eligibility/duration vary. Health insurance can leave premiums, deductibles, copays, coinsurance, noncovered and out-of-network costs. Homeowners policies have deductibles, exclusions and claim timing. These protections reduce some risks but do not make months-long cash needs rare for every household."", ""RECOMMENDATION BOUNDARY: A tiered fund and balance between debt repayment, cash and investment opportunity can be reasonable, but the correct allocation depends on liquidity needs and risk tolerance. No universal optimal emergency-fund amount, debt sequence or investment comparison is established by the public evidence.""], ""ending_words"": ""Stop letting perfect be the enemy of good and start building your safety net today. Your future self will thank you when life inevitably decides to get expensive at the worst possible moment."", ""opening_words"": ""Picture this. You're lying in bed at 3:00 in the morning staring at the ceiling wondering if you could survive financially if your car decided to transform into an expensive pile of metal tomorrow."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_STORED_TEXT"", ""source"": {""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::329_RmJKnJkUgxA_How_Much_Emergency_Cash_You_Actually_Need_Saved.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""64c45c42a1d8468a96646ca6660e658e4d1874dc704afbf31cc120d29b7dac38"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""Words"": ""3206""}, ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""audit-batches/NICK_157_READ_RECEIPTS.json""], ""stored_text_completeness"": ""FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverif