b5886f08e5"", ""image_source"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/SJLyXAGbBLk/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""mobile_review_width"": 320, ""performance_effect"": ""NOT_ESTABLISHED"", ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_IMAGE"", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""THUMBNAIL_COVERAGE.csv:SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""audit-batches/NICK_159_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""The narrative opens with novelty and a relatable anxiety, earns credibility by challenging its own headline, then moves from history to behavioral mechanisms and a usable routine. Callbacks to Hani's memory slogan and concrete purchase examples create cohesion. The practical system is clear, but evidence from note-taking, memory generation and workplace goal accountability is repeatedly transferred to budgeting outcomes without a direct Kakeibo trial. The script also contrasts finance-app usage with aggregate debt as though contemporaneous movement demonstrated failure; those series do not identify causation."", ""audio_verified"": false, ""availability"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""beat_map"": [""1904 system and late-night banking-anxiety hook"", ""Immediate disclaimer of the 35% headline"", ""Motoko Hani origin story"", ""Budget-app retention and debt contrast"", ""Pain-of-paying and manual-friction mechanism"", ""Handwriting, generation-effect and goal-writing evidence"", ""Monthly setup and four spending categories"", ""Four reflection questions and 24-hour pause"", ""Restriction versus attention philosophy"", ""Step-by-step implementation"", ""Invisible-spending payoff and historical callback""], ""claim_audit"": [""CORRECTLY DISCLAIMED: The 35% figure is not presented as a research result. No controlled Kakeibo savings study or defined denominator is supplied, so it cannot support a guaranteed or typical wealth increase."", ""PARTLY SUPPORTED HISTORY: Japan's National Diet Library supports Hani as Japan's first woman journalist and the 1903 launch of Katei no Tomo. The transcript's claim that she held that status 'in 1904' is imprecise because she joined Hochi-sha in 1897. The 1904 ledger origin is widely documented, but exact original questions, categories, slogan continuity and unchanged bookstore form were not verified from a primary 1904 artifact."", ""CATEGORY MISMATCH: A commercial analytics source reports roughly 9% day-30 retention for the broad finance-app category, not specifically budgeting apps and not the script's full 4-9% range. The 84% annual abandonment figure was not reproduced from a named cohort."", ""NON-CAUSAL DEBT COMPARISON: New York Fed data can measure household and card balances, but rising aggregate debt does not show that budgeting apps failed. Latest releases relative to the March 2026 upload, definitions and release dates must be stated; later Q1/Q2 2026 values cannot be silently treated as publication-era evidence."", ""UNREPRODUCED POPULATION CLAIMS: The 65% paycheck-to-paycheck and 42% no-savings figures lack named surveys, populations, dates and definitions in the script."", ""SUPPORTED EXPERIMENT, OVEREXTENDED APPLICATION: A 2024 36-student EEG study found more elaborate connectivity during stylus handwriting than single-finger typing. It did not test budgets, saving or financial behavior, so it cannot establish that a paper ledger outperforms an app."", ""REPLICATION LIMIT: The original longhand note-taking result is not settled. Direct replications and mini meta-analyses found no reliable immediate-learning advantage, so 'consistently outperformed' is too categorical."", ""SUPPORTED MEMORY EFFECT, NOT BUDGET EFFECT: The generation-effect meta-analysis covered 86 studies and estimated about 0.40 standard deviations for generated versus read material, with substantial moderator variation. It did not establish that manually recording purchases reduces repeat spending."", ""CONFOUNDED GOAL COMPARISON: Dominican University's summary says 267 people were recruited, but the >70% group sent weekly updates to a friend while the 35% group kept goals private without writing. The comparison combines writing, action commitments and accountability; it does not show that writing alone doubled achievement."", ""PRACTICAL GUIDANCE, NOT GUARANTEE: Recording spending, setting an explicit savings target and pausing before optional purchases can support awareness. The proposed 5%/10% goals, four-way weekly division and 24-hour rule are heuristics, not universally validated outcomes or individualized financial advice.""], ""ending_words"": ""You need a few minutes a day, a savings goal you actually write down, and the habit of asking yourself once a month how you can improve. That's the whole thing. If this video made something click, subscribe and hit the like button. It tells the algorithm this is worth spreading, and if you want to see what happens when you take those savings and actually put them to work, the video on compound interest is right here. I'll see you there."", ""opening_words"": ""In 1904, Japan's first female journalist invented a budgeting system that people still buy unchanged in bookstores today. No app, no algorithm, just paper, a pen, and four questions. It's called Kakeibo."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_STORED_TEXT"", ""source"": {""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::182_SJLyXAGbBLk_A_Simple_Japanese_Money_Trick_to_Become_35_Richer.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""9eb919f3b238af3e18a3736c98d1f8f12343b15088da9c86d06e1551b18601c9"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""Words"": ""3458""}, ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""audit-batches/NICK_159_READ_RECEIPTS.json""], ""stored_text_completeness"": ""FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated cap