ript acknowledges geography and age but repeatedly returns to a universal rank it cannot calculate. The frozen result is 0.32349x WEAK against ten peers and 0.27962x WEAK against five; public counts do not establish a title effect."", ""peer_comparison_ids"": [""zutCmVPrQcU"", ""N3vR3PIIyQI"", ""4KiTtL0OrPs"", ""30EYlADmKpE"", ""AFQ0VrhqDgY"", ""SlyosXgW__4"", ""4D90YRoT2yo"", ""Ygv_u2j90OA"", ""uU64zaQKd30"", ""7ldkoTCBS_4""], ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_TITLE"", ""script_alignment"": ""The script supplies many median and average benchmarks, but it never defines or computes one composite top-half measure. Several figures use the wrong population, date or denominator."", ""search_demand"": ""Broad social-comparison and financial-benchmark intent is inferred; historical query volume, recommendation exposure and traffic sources are unavailable."", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""audit-batches/NICK_161_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""The actual 1280x720 image uses huge black 'TOP 50% OR NOT?' text, a skeptical cartoon figure, green coins and a small house versus a broken pink piggy bank, plus a thick arrow. At 320x180 the question, face, green wealth stack and broken pig remain legible. The binary green-versus-broken framing overstates a multidimensional script and may imply homeowners or visible assets define rank. Historical art, impressions, CTR and tests are unavailable."", ""coverage"": {""Association_verified"": ""NO"", ""Historical_design"": ""UNKNOWN"", ""Local_asset"": """", ""Mobile_review"": ""NOT_REVIEWED"", ""Thumbnail_URL"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/SU-3ZcgmCJ4/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""URL_status"": ""PRESENT_NOT_FETCHED"", ""Video_ID"": ""SU-3ZcgmCJ4""}, ""dimensions"": [1280, 720], ""historical_design"": ""UNKNOWN"", ""image_path"": ""research/finance-economics-intelligence/audit-batches/NICK_SU-3ZcgmCJ4.jpg"", ""image_sha256"": ""5504e0427e7eb9b65e3171ef296be0217a9f3ed7db5a5ba3ceb906fdca5a4493"", ""image_source"": ""https://i.ytimg.com/vi/SU-3ZcgmCJ4/maxresdefault.jpg"", ""mobile_review_width"": 320, ""performance_effect"": ""NOT_ESTABLISHED"", ""review_scope"": ""ACTUAL_IMAGE"", ""source_refs"": [""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""THUMBNAIL_COVERAGE.csv:SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""audit-batches/NICK_161_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]}","{""analysis"": ""The script opens with identity anxiety, promises an exact relative rank, then moves metric by metric through income, wealth, liquidity, debt, housing, retirement and saving. Rhetorical questions, 'shocking' reversals, concrete household vignettes and repeated relief-versus-panic framing maintain an information gap. The conclusion pivots usefully from current rank to trajectory, but the narrative never reconciles its measures into one rank and treats aggregates as individual norms. Several auto-caption values are visibly corrupted, while other errors remain substantive even if audio were perfect. The advice to track spending and build buffers is general; the claimed statistical precision is not."", ""audio_verified"": false, ""availability"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""beat_map"": [""Social-comparison hook and subscribe prompt"", ""National income median and middle-class band"", ""Net-worth definition and national median"", ""Age-specific net-worth benchmarks"", ""Three-month emergency resources"", ""Aggregate debt and debt-service burden"", ""Credit-card and medical-debt warnings"", ""Housing burden and $250,000-home claim"", ""Massachusetts-versus-Mississippi geography"", ""Defined-contribution balances"", ""Retirement targets and Social Security"", ""Personal-saving-rate claim"", ""Geographic arbitrage anecdotes"", ""Savings-rate trajectory and closing motivation""], ""claim_audit"": [""INCOME SUPPORTED, CLASS BAND NOT OFFICIAL: Census reported 2024 real median household income of $83,730 before the September 23 publication. Half above and half below is correct for that distribution. The stated $53,740-$161,220 middle-class band does not equal two-thirds to double $83,730, which is $55,820-$167,460, and 'middle class' is not an official Census cutoff."", ""NET-WORTH DATE AND UNIT ERROR: The Federal Reserve SCF reports 2022 median family net worth of $192,900, not a 2023 household estimate of $192,084. Its comparable 2019 value in 2022 dollars was $141,100, not $121,411. Cross-source inflation bases may explain some variants, but the script does not disclose them."", ""AGE MEDIANS MATERIALLY WRONG: The SCF age medians are $39,000 under 35, $135,600 ages 35-44, $247,200 ages 45-54, $364,500 ages 55-64 and $409,900 ages 65-74. Only the first figure roughly matches. The canonical caption's '212,166,000' is impossible and original audio was not inspected."", ""EMERGENCY SPLIT SUPPORTED: The 2024 SHED supports 55% with three months of rainy-day savings, another 15% able to cover by borrowing, asset sales or other savings, and 30% unable by any means. These are adults' self-reports, not audited household balance sheets."", ""DEBT DENOMINATOR FAILURE: The New York Fed supports $18.388 trillion total household debt in Q2 2025 and $1.209 trillion credit-card balances. It does not support $15,056 per household. Even a crude division by about 132 million households produces roughly $139,000, and mean aggregate debt is not typical household debt. The later sentence says over $100,000, contradicting the caption's $15,056."", ""DEBT-SERVICE INTERPRETATION OVERSTATED: A roughly 11.1% aggregate debt-service ratio is plausible, but it is required payments divided by aggregate disposable personal income. It does not show every indebted household is managing better, and methodological changes complicate long comparisons."", ""CREDIT-CARD STORY MORALIZES WITHOUT EVIDENCE: Aggregate revolving balances and high rates do not establish that balances mainly represent forgotten dinners, unused clothes or impulse purchases. Households also use cards for necessities and shocks. The transcript supplies no primary source for $10,815 among revolving households."", ""MEDICAL-DEBT CLAIM UNREPRODUCED: The $220 billion and 40% claims are not supported by the cited federal sources located for this review. SHED reports 15%-18% of adults with outstanding medical debt in recent years; broader definitions can differ, so source, unit and definition are required."", ""HOUSING CLAIMS FALSELY GENERALIZED: Census reports 46.3% of renters cost-burdened and 23.9% severely burdened in 2023, while homeowners have lower rates. This does not support 76% of all Americans over 31% or more than half over 50%. The 30% benchmark is a burden definition, not an individual affordability law."", ""STATE FIGURES CORRUPTED OR WRONG: Official 2023 ACS medians were $99,858 in Massachusetts and $54,203 in Mississippi. The canonical caption renders $16,500 and $441,966, then later $16,000 and $44,900. Original audio is unavailable, so exact spoken values cannot be certified; the transcript still cannot support the claimed arithmetic."", ""HOME-AFFORDABILITY CLAIM BLOCKED: No named source or disclosed interest rate, down payment, taxes, insurance, debts or underwriting method is given for 66.6 million households unable to afford a $250,000 home. Affordability cannot be inferred from price alone."", ""401(K) POPULATION BOUNDARY: Vanguard supports a $148,153 average and $38,176 median among nearly five million participants in its defined-contribution plans. This is not all Americans and excludes other plans and assets. Current-plan balances are partial preparedness measures; the transcript's 20s and 50s age examples do not match Vanguard's table."", ""RETIREMENT-SAVINGS CLAIM REVERSED/UNSUPPORTED: Federal Reserve evidence says 67% of adults had a tax-preferred retirement account or pension in 2024. The claim that 54% had no retirement savings is not supported and may invert a statistic about ownership or plan type."", ""SOCIAL SECURITY ERROR: SSA estimated $1,976 monthly for all retired workers in January 2025, not $1,550. Benefit adequacy depends on household composition, claiming age, other income and local costs."", ""SAVING-RATE CATEGORY ERROR: BEA's November 2024 vintage showed 4.4%, while the later December release showed 3.8%. The personal saving rate is aggregate personal saving divided by aggregate disposable income, not the percentage saved by the average person. Saving 10% does not by itself prove top-half financial position."", ""CAUSAL CLAIMS UNSOURCED: The assertions that consistent 10% savers climb wealth rankings and lifestyle inflators slide down are plausible hypotheses, not results established by the cited cross-sectional figures. Income, returns, inheritance, housing, health, family size, taxes and shocks also affect rank.""], ""ending_words"": ""So, there you have it. Whether you're crushing it financially or barely keeping your head above water, at least now you know exactly where you stand compared to everyone else. Use these numbers as motivation, not as an excuse to panic or get complacent. Your financial future depends on what you do next, not where you started."", ""opening_words"": ""Are you secretly crushing it financially without even realizing it? Or are you part of the struggling masses who think everyone else has their money figured out? The answer might shock you because most Americans have absolutely no clue where they actually stand compared to their neighbors, co-workers, and that guy on Instagram who somehow affords a Tesla on a barista salary."", ""review_scope"": ""FULL_STORED_TEXT"", ""source"": {""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::336_SU-3ZcgmCJ4_Are_You_In_The_Top_50_Of_Americans_Financially_Surprising_Data.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""85c30461e0fcd9521662c764747fd6e9b812834c1545e8537b7a2a026a3